\paragraph{Embedding similarity and format independence.}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space: unconditional pairwise $R^2$ is 1.3\%, rising to 7\% conditional on nonzero confusion; correlated aggregation amplifies this to batch-level $R^2$ of 18--25\% (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}).
The batch-level BCS--performance relationship holds across all RE settings (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}; \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) but not NER ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$). A paired constrained vs.\ unconstrained comparison confirms format-independence (Fisher $z = -0.04$, $p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:format-independence}), indicating confusability-driven degradation is intrinsic to type semantic overlap. The task asymmetry reflects error-mode structure: RE errors concentrate in type substitutions \citep{tam-2024-let-me-speak}, whereas NER errors scatter across missed entities, boundary misalignment, and type confusion \citep{schall-2025-hidden-cost}. A relaxed-matching evaluation corroborates this: removing boundary errors improves NER F1 by 4.2\,pp but leaves the BCS correlation unchanged ($r = 0.003$; Appendix~\ref{app:relaxed-matching}). SCS likely proxies deeper conceptual overlap: a renaming experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:renaming}) reduced surface similarity by 0.20 without reducing confusion. We hypothesize that high-SCS type pairs occupy nearby regions in the LLM's output embedding space, intensifying softmax competition during token generation---a prediction testable via logit-space analysis in future work.

A natural intervention against confusability-related degradation is to partition the schema so confusable types never co-occur. We test this with \textbf{Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition} (SDGD), which applies greedy graph coloring on the SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches.

\paragraph{NER and ED: merge amplification.}
All decomposition modes fall below the single-pass NER baseline (F1\,=\,0.445): SDGD obtains 0.251, random batching 0.320. The core failure is \emph{merge amplification}: presenting text $k$ times collapses precision (0.563$\to$0.210--0.249) as false positives scale with $k$. MAVEN event detection (168 types) replicates the pattern (precision 0.287$\to$0.091, 3.7$\times$ inflation; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}).

